id: rust.raii
kind: rust
title: RAII and Drop
aliases: RAII, Drop, guards
tags: ownership, resource management
questions:
  - how do you guarantee a resource is released in Rust?
invariant: Destructors run when a value leaves scope, on early return and on panic unwind, so release logic belongs in Drop.
answer_30s: RAII ties release to scope: locks, permits, transactions and file handles release when their guard drops, including on error and panic paths.
answer_2m: |
  This is why the async primitives are shaped the way they are: a
  SemaphorePermit, a MutexGuard and a transaction guard all release on drop, so
  cancellation and error paths cannot leak the resource. Two honest limits:
  Drop does not run on process abort (crash cleanup is the orchestrator's job),
  and Drop must not block or await. For explicit ordering, scope blocks and
  drop() calls are the tool.
when_to_use:
  - locks, permits, transactions, temp files, tracing spans
avoid_when:
  - hiding blocking or fallible cleanup in Drop (use an explicit close called on both paths)
minimal_example: |
  let permit = semaphore.clone().acquire_owned().await?; // acquired
  do_work().await?;                                      // may fail or be cancelled
  drop(permit);                                          // released, always
failure_modes:
  - relying on Drop for durable cleanup that a process kill would skip
  - blocking work inside Drop on a runtime thread
related:
  - rust.tokio-semaphore
  - rust.mutex
  - rust.ownership-move
sources:
  - https://doc.rust-lang.org/std/ops/trait.Drop.html
